#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116080} :activity/id #uuid "dc1672e0-ddb3-52e3-a848-0b32527e980b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049670 :topic/id #uuid "097a5479-f143-5c43-bf8b-9a19a9b5adef" :topic/math-academy-id 1643} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116482 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation that has logarithms with the same base on both sides, we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by the base. For example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3(2x-4)=\\log_3(x+3).\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are represented as logarithms of the same base ($3$), we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4)} & ={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ 2x-4 & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =x+3 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is the solution.\n\n**Caution:** Remember that, when solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get *extraneous* solutions that don't actually satisfy the original equation. We can't take a logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\nSo, once we've found the solutions, we need to substitute them back into the original equation and check that the arguments of the logarithms are positive. Substituting $x=7$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(x+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 7-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(7+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(10) & ={log}_{3}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is a valid solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "c314730c-59d5-5d55-a97a-f76c72c50e44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2823 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14f6916c-9a23-5153-9ef6-f85e87dd6b7a" :step/math-academy-id 14956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116211}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115040 :question/id #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547058}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"} :step/id #uuid "2ffbd10a-703d-5692-83b3-f10a8a0d288c" :step/math-academy-id 14958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115042 :question/id #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" :question/math-academy-id "e-4150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050497}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546959}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "21cf2aa1-ffe8-5601-b955-05841186df3f" :step/math-academy-id 14961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115043 :question/id #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547065}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"} :step/id #uuid "5bfc64b7-a52a-5d81-be3f-a6fb0a1cc0ff" :step/math-academy-id 14968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116211 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116523 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, you may have noticed that for the logarithmic equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 (2x-4) = \\log_3 (x+3),\n$$\n\nwe were able to \"cancel out\" the logarithms and equate the arguments:\n\n$$\n2x-4 = x+3\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. Whenever the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments.\n\n**Caution:** This trick only works when the logarithms have the same base. The equation must take the form\n\n$$\n\\log_n f(x) = \\log_n g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the base of *both* logarithms and $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are the arguments of the logarithms." :tutorial/id #uuid "89c2d718-2d9b-59b8-b758-2c4a019a2313" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4224 :tutorial/title "Equating Arguments in a Logarithmic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "4186d791-eca5-5bfb-9ba0-118f5d95b418" :step/math-academy-id 25610 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116081}}] :activity/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Logarithms on Both Sides" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115443}]}
